---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/klaviyo-vs-drip';
const title = 'Klaviyo vs Drip: Shopify events, workflows and SMS access';
const desc = 'Compare Klaviyo and Drip through account eligibility, Shopify identity, catalog events, purchase exits and maintainable campaign queues.';
const faqs = [
  { question: 'When does Klaviyo fit?', answer: 'Consider Klaviyo when a team can maintain its Shopify event and catalog mappings and coordinate eligible email and SMS journeys. Confirm destination coverage, sender approval, purchased features and data requirements before depending on a branch or predictive segment.' },
  { question: 'When does Drip fit?', answer: 'Drip can fit an email-led team that values maintaining its existing workflows. Native SMS requires confirmed account access; its current rollout has US, paid-plan, Support All People and email-linked identity restrictions. An SMS-only audience needs a different design.' },
  { question: 'Does a shared profile grant permission for both channels?', answer: 'No. Keep permission by channel and purpose, with capture source, disclosure and timestamp. Reconcile opt-outs and pending sends without treating an email subscription or purchase as promotional SMS consent.' },
  { question: 'How should a migration be tested?', answer: 'Use controlled profiles to trace multi-item orders, returning identities, opt-outs, delayed purchases and replayed events. Keep one live sender per message job, inspect pending queues and reconcile orders and suppressions before transferring ownership.' },
  { question: 'Which costs less?', answer: 'Obtain account-specific quotes for the same audience and send mix. Compare contribution per eligible customer against a holdout after product costs, messaging, platform and operating costs; count discounts and returns once in net sales.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <nav><a href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Klaviyo vs Drip</nav>
    <h1>Klaviyo vs Drip: compare the event-to-send workflow</h1>
    <p>Choose with a Shopify order that contains products with different replenishment needs, then changes before a reminder leaves the queue. This comparison proposes acceptance checks; it reports no merchant results or universal winner.</p>
    <TldrBox title="Conditional fits" picks={[
      { label: 'Klaviyo', tool: 'Event-led coordination', reason: 'Consider when the team can maintain profile, catalog and flow contracts and its required channels are enabled.' },
      { label: 'Drip', tool: 'Email-led workflow continuity', reason: 'Consider when existing journeys remain maintainable and any required SMS access is confirmed.' },
    ]} />
    <h2>Start with actual channel access</h2>
    <p><a href="https://www.drip.com/sms">Drip’s SMS rollout page</a>, checked November 25, 2026, limits requests to existing US-based paid customers with Support All People enabled. Phone records require email; SMS-only subscribers are unsupported. It describes staged access, toll-free one-way sending and no keyword-to-join, short codes, two-way replies or RCS at launch. Confirm activation in your account before planning around native SMS. Older help pages still describe SMS as unavailable to new users; a phone field does not prove sending access.</p>
    <p><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/4402914866843">Klaviyo’s country guide</a> makes SMS availability destination-dependent. Check recipient country, sender type, registration and your account’s rates separately. Email availability does not establish SMS coverage, and a broader channel menu does not establish entitlement to every feature. Verify purchased plans, usage limits and data eligibility for predictive or catalog-dependent features instead of buying on a feature label.</p>
    <h2>Compare the same Shopify contract</h2>
    <p>In <a href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a>, inspect the actual profile, event properties and flow entry; in <a href="/apps/drip">Drip</a>, inspect the person record, incoming event and workflow branch. A shared business label such as “purchased” is insufficient. Record the Shopify customer and order identifiers, event time, product and variant identifiers, quantity, currency and subscription state. Check which fields your installed integration actually supplies and how refunds, cancellations and guest checkouts update them.</p>
    <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Acceptance task</th><th>Klaviyo inspection</th><th>Drip inspection</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Returning shopper changes email</td><td>Profile association and duplicate flow entries</td><td>Person association and email-linked phone record</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Multi-item order</td><td>Line-item properties and catalog lookup used by each branch</td><td>Event fields and product rules used by each branch</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Purchase during delay</td><td>Pending flow messages and dispatch eligibility</td><td>Pending workflow messages and dispatch eligibility</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Replayed event after reconnect</td><td>Entry history and duplicate-message prevention</td><td>Workflow history and duplicate-message prevention</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <h2>Keep distinct campaign jobs distinct</h2>
    <p>For limited sauce batches or art editions, propose a launch notice followed by a stock-based notice only if inventory still supports it. For pottery or size-specific sneaker waitlists, preserve signup priority and variant availability; a marketing queue is not an inventory reservation. For one-of-one denim, test style preferences and remove sold listings before dispatch.</p>
    <p>For coffee subscriptions, suppress active subscribers from acquisition discounts while evaluating replenishment separately. For audio launches, compare an education sequence with a discount offer using contribution, rather than assuming an incentive is necessary. For sports-nutrition collaborations, keep an email bundle offer and an SMS timing notice within the same offer and frequency policy.</p>
    <p>For surfwear restocks, use the requested product waitlist rather than a full-list announcement. For streetwear drops, test early access against fulfillment capacity and cumulative contact pressure. For tour merchandise, validate city and recipient timezone instead of using venue time as a proxy for customer location.</p>
    <p>For record collectors, distinguish repeat purchases from genuine category interest before assigning early access. For candle gifting, distinguish gifts from self-purchases and use real shipping cutoffs. For outdoor clearance, compare bargain-sensitive audiences with full-price loyalists under a holdout. These are proposed scenarios, not named customer case studies or measured gains.</p>
    <h2>Permission, suppression and purchase exits</h2>
    <p>A profile join must preserve channel and purpose permissions. Retain capture source, timestamp and disclosure version; a purchase, email opt-in or imported phone number does not authorize promotional texts. Apply the relevant recipient rules and quiet hours with qualified review, including uncertain timezone cases. See <a href="/guides/consent">consent</a> and <a href="/guides/tcpa">messaging requirements</a>.</p>
    <p>Test an opt-out while a message is waiting, a purchase before a cart reminder, a refund after a replenishment event and a subscription change before a flash offer. Confirm the intended exit at dispatch as well as at entry. Record channel-specific opt-outs separately from broader suppression requests, and verify each destination receives the required update. Do not erase valid email permission merely to model SMS status, or retain promotional SMS eligibility after withdrawal.</p>
    <h2>One queue owner through migration and failure</h2>
    <p>Assign a named owner to each live campaign and automation, its pending queue, reply route and suppression sync. Where Drip’s one-way SMS scope is insufficient for support, supply a clearly owned support path rather than promising conversational service. Adding Postscript, Attentive or Sequenzy is a separate architecture decision, not a default requirement; retain one owner for each message job and verify connectors before duplicating journeys.</p>
    <p>Rehearse connector downtime, duplicate events, late orders and out-of-order updates with controlled records. Preserve event identifiers and timestamps, reconcile source orders to destination histories and establish a restart watermark. Before replay, determine which events are already processed and which queued notices are obsolete. Pause the affected job if eligibility cannot be established; transferring ownership must not release both old and rebuilt queues.</p>
    <h2>Cost and evidence before a decision</h2>
    <p>Request quotes for the same audience, destinations, peak send mix, encoding, message segments and required features. Include email and SMS subscriptions, overages, applicable carrier charges, connectors, support and migration labor. Confirm what is included rather than assuming every fee applies to both products. See <a href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a>.</p>
    <p>For a controlled comparison, calculate contribution as net sales after discounts and returns, minus product, fulfillment, messaging, platform and operating costs. Avoid subtracting discounts or returns again. Compare contribution per eligible customer with the same denominator in treatment and holdout; platform-attributed revenue alone does not establish lift. Choose the observation window for the buying and return cycle and sufficient evidence, rather than a fixed pilot duration.</p>
    <p>Choose Klaviyo if its verified data contracts and enabled channels make your journeys easier to maintain. Choose Drip if workflow continuity and confirmed access meet the brief with less operating effort. Keep the current setup when neither candidate passes identity, permission, queue and reconciliation checks. Preserve the traces and unresolved gaps so a future feature or plan change can be evaluated against the same brief.</p>
  </article>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Klaviyo vs Drip FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
